  "helpSectionWhy": "Why does it matter?",
  "helpSectionWhere": "Where to find this info?",
  "helpPillarSystemTitle": "3 pillar system",
  "helpPillarSystemExplanation": "Switzerland organizes retirement in 3 levels: a basic pension (OASI), occupational pension (BVG), and private savings (3a). Together, they aim to maintain your standard of living.",
  "helpPillarSystemWhy": "Understanding this system helps you identify what you can optimize for your retirement.",
  "helpPillarSystemWhere": "Your salary certificate and annual pension statement detail your contributions.",
  "helpPillar1AvsTitle": "OASI (1st pillar)",
  "helpPillar1AvsExplanation": "OASI is the basic pension everyone receives at retirement. It's funded by your salary contributions (deducted automatically) and your employer's contributions.",
  "helpPillar1AvsWhy": "OASI alone only covers about 40% of your last salary. That's why the 2nd and 3rd pillars are essential.",
  "helpPillar1AvsWhere": "Request an OASI account statement from your cantonal compensation office website.",
  "helpPillar2BvgTitle": "BVG / 2nd pillar",
  "helpPillar2BvgExplanation": "Occupational pension is mandatory savings managed by your employer. You and your employer contribute monthly. This capital accumulates and is paid out at retirement.",
  "helpPillar2BvgWhy": "It's often the largest amount in your retirement. Check your annual pension certificate to know your capital.",
  "helpPillar2BvgWhere": "Your annual pension certificate, sent by your employer's pension fund.",
  "helpPillar3aTitle": "Pillar 3a",
  "helpPillar3aExplanation": "Pillar 3a is voluntary savings you manage yourself. You choose your provider, amount, and investment type. The money is locked until retirement (with exceptions).",
  "helpPillar3aWhy": "Every franc contributed to 3a is tax-deductible. It's the simplest way to pay less tax while preparing for retirement.",
  "helpPillar3aWhere": "Log in to your 3a provider's website (bank or app) to see your balance.",
  "helpCoordinatedSalaryTitle": "Coordinated salary",
  "helpCoordinatedSalaryExplanation": "It's the portion of your salary on which your BVG contributions are calculated. A fixed amount (coordination deduction) is subtracted from your gross salary.",
  "helpCoordinatedSalaryWhy": "The higher it is, the larger your contributions and your future pension will be.",
  "helpCoordinatedSalaryWhere": "Shown on your annual BVG pension certificate.",
  "helpConversionRateTitle": "Conversion rate",
  "helpConversionRateExplanation": "This percentage converts your BVG capital into an annual pension. For example, with 6.8% and CHF 500,000 capital, you receive CHF 34,000 per year.",
  "helpConversionRateWhy": "A higher rate = a better pension. The legal minimum is 6.8%, but funds can apply a lower rate on the non-mandatory portion.",
  "helpConversionRateWhere": "Shown on your annual pension certificate or your pension fund's regulations.",
  "helpBvgCapitalTitle": "BVG capital",
  "helpBvgCapitalExplanation": "This is the money accumulated in your pension fund (2nd pillar). Your and your employer's contributions add up monthly, plus interest.",
  "helpBvgCapitalWhy": "It's usually your largest asset. It directly determines your pension amount at retirement.",
  "helpBvgCapitalWhere": "Your annual pension certificate, section 'retirement assets'.",
  "helpReplacementRateTitle": "Replacement rate",
  "helpReplacementRateExplanation": "The percentage of your last salary you'll receive in retirement. For example, 65% means if you earn CHF 100,000, your pension will be about CHF 65,000 per year.",
  "helpReplacementRateWhy": "The goal is typically 60-80%. Below 60%, your standard of living may drop significantly in retirement.",
  "helpReplacementRateWhere": "PocketPillar calculates it for you from your data. You can also ask your pension fund.",
  "helpPensionGapTitle": "Pension gap",
  "helpPensionGapExplanation": "The difference between the pension you should receive by law and what you'll actually receive. If your employer contributes the legal minimum, the gap may be zero.",
  "helpPensionGapWhy": "A positive gap means you're below the legal minimum and could indicate an issue with your contributions.",
  "helpPensionGapWhere": "Compare your pension certificate with BVG minimums, or use the PocketPillar calculator.",
  "helpTaxSavings3aTitle": "3a tax savings",
  "helpTaxSavings3aExplanation": "Every franc contributed to your 3a reduces your taxable income. Depending on your canton and income, you can save between CHF 1,500 and CHF 3,000 in taxes per year.",
  "helpTaxSavings3aWhy": "It's money you keep instead of giving to the tax office. The more you earn, the greater the savings.",
  "helpTaxSavings3aWhere": "Use PocketPillar's tax calculator by selecting your canton.",
  "helpBvgBuybackTitle": "BVG buyback",
  "helpBvgBuybackExplanation": "A voluntary payment into your 2nd pillar to fill contribution gaps. For example, if you didn't work in Switzerland for a few years.",
  "helpBvgBuybackWhy": "The amount is 100% tax-deductible in the year of payment. It's a very effective tax strategy.",
  "helpBvgBuybackWhere": "Your pension certificate shows the maximum possible buyback amount. Contact your pension fund.",
  "helpRetirementAgeTitle": "Retirement age",
  "helpRetirementAgeExplanation": "In Switzerland, the reference retirement age is 65 (AHV 21 transition for women born 1961-1963: 64.5 years in 2026). You can take early retirement from 58 or defer until 70.",
  "helpRetirementAgeWhy": "Each year of early retirement reduces your pension. Each year of deferral increases it. It's an important financial decision.",
  "helpRetirementAgeWhere": "FSIO website (Federal Social Insurance Office) or your cantonal compensation office.",
  "helpContribution3aMaxTitle": "3a maximum",
  "helpContribution3aMaxExplanation": "The maximum 3a contribution is set by law. In 2026, it's CHF 7,258 with a 2nd pillar, or CHF 36,288 without (max 20% of net income).",
  "helpContribution3aMaxWhy": "Contributing the maximum is almost always beneficial: you maximize your tax savings.",
  "helpContribution3aMaxWhere": "The amount is published annually by the FSIO. PocketPillar is always up to date.",
  "helpGrossIncomeTitle": "Gross income",
  "helpGrossIncomeExplanation": "Your annual salary before any deductions (taxes, OASI, BVG, etc.). It's the amount shown on your employment contract.",
  "helpGrossIncomeWhy": "It's the basis for calculating your contributions and retirement projections.",
  "helpGrossIncomeWhere": "Your employment contract, monthly payslip, or annual salary certificate.",
  "helpEffectiveReturnTitle": "Effective return",
  "helpEffectiveReturnExplanation": "The actual return on your 3a contribution, accounting for tax savings. It's like an immediate bonus on your investment.",
  "helpEffectiveReturnWhy": "An effective return of 30% means for CHF 7,258 contributed, you get back about CHF 2,177 in tax savings.",
  "helpEffectiveReturnWhere": "Use PocketPillar's tax calculator to see your effective return based on your canton.",

  "tipMax3a2026Title": "3a maximum 2026",
  "tipMax3a2026Body": "The 3a maximum for 2026 is CHF 7,258. Contribute before December 31 to save on your taxes!",
  "tipBvgBuybackTitle": "BVG buyback = double savings",
  "tipBvgBuybackBody": "A BVG buyback is 100% tax-deductible AND increases your pension. Ask your fund about your buyback potential.",
  "tip3aTaxDeductionTitle": "3a reduces your taxes",
  "tip3aTaxDeductionBody": "Every franc contributed to 3a is deductible from your taxable income. Depending on your canton, that can mean over 30% immediate return!",
  "tipStartEarlyTitle": "Start early",
  "tipStartEarlyBody": "Starting 3a savings at 25 instead of 35 can earn you over CHF 100,000 more thanks to compound interest.",
  "tipCompoundInterestTitle": "The magic of compound interest",
  "tipCompoundInterestBody": "Your interest earns interest too. Over 30 years, a 3a investment at 3% return nearly doubles your invested capital.",
  "tipMultiple3aTitle": "Multiple 3a accounts",
  "tipMultiple3aBody": "Opening multiple 3a accounts (up to 5) allows staggered withdrawals and reduces capital withdrawal tax.",
  "tipRetirementGapTitle": "The retirement gap",
  "tipRetirementGapBody": "On average, the 1st and 2nd pillars only cover 60% of your last salary. 3a is essential to close this gap.",
  "tip3PillarsTitle": "Why 3 pillars?",
  "tip3PillarsBody": "The Swiss system spreads risk: the state (OASI), the employer (BVG), and yourself (3a). Each plays a role in your financial security.",
  "tipAvsMaxTitle": "Maximum OASI pension",
  "tipAvsMaxBody": "The maximum OASI pension is CHF 2,520/month for a single person (2026). Even high earners are capped at this amount.",
  "tipPillar2InterestTitle": "BVG interest rate",
  "tipPillar2InterestBody": "Your mandatory BVG capital earns at least 1.25% per year. Some funds offer more on the non-mandatory portion.",
  "tip3aWithdrawalTitle": "Early 3a withdrawal",
  "tip3aWithdrawalBody": "You can withdraw your 3a before retirement to buy a home, become self-employed, or leave Switzerland.",
  "tipCantonTaxesTitle": "The canton impact",
  "tipCantonTaxesBody": "The 3a tax savings vary enormously by canton. In Geneva, it can be 2x higher than in Zug on the same income.",

